condition_name: Asphyxiated Babies
condition_slug: asphyxiated-babies
source: NSTG 2022
introduction: ""
clinical_features[1]:
  - type: manifestations
    features[8]: Inability to cry or suck,Global hypotonia and poor activity or global hypertonia with brisk deep tendon reflexes,Shock and oliguria,Poor temperature regulation,Paralytic ileus,Respiratory distress,Bleeding tendencies,Seizures
investigations[5]: Oxygen saturation via pulse oximetry,Random blood glucose,"Serum electrolytes, urea and creatinine, serum calcium",Serum troponin,"Serum aspartate aminotransferase, serum alanine aminotransferase"
treatment:
  goals[0]:
  non_drug[6]: Serial monitoring of parameters,Frequent airway clearing and ventilation by bag and mask,Nil per oral should be maintained until peristalsis is present and respiratory rate is normal,Feeding with expressed breast milk via nasogastric or orogastric tube,Monitor urine output using diaper weight or catheterization,Invite nephrologist if oliguria is noted
  drug[4]: Seizures managed with slow intravenous phenobarbitone,Use inotropes if hypotension is not corrected by fluid bolus (dobutamine or dopamine),Administer magnesium sulphate,Oxygen administration
  adverse_reactions_and_cautions[0]:
  supportive_measures[7]: Prevent over-hydration by administering exact daily maintenance fluid requirement,Observe for signs of hypovolaemia and hypoperfusion (blood pressure/capillary refill time),Provide fluid bolus of normal saline if circulatory collapse occurs,Serial serum monitoring of glucose and treat hypoglycaemia,Correct electrolyte derangement,"Avoid hypoxaemia, maintain SpO₂ >94%",Selective head cooling if available
differential_diagnoses[0]:
complications[0]:
prevention[0]:
other_investigations: null
definitive_treatment: null
prognosis[1]: The persistence of abnormal cry and abnormal motor functions beyond the first week of life highly suggests severe cerebral damage.